steps.body:
Each node holds a Map from a letter to the child node for that letter, and a flag that says whether a word ends there. The root is an empty node that stands for the empty prefix.

```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.children = new Map();
    this.isEnd = false;
  }
}
console.log(new TrieNode().children.size); // 0, no letters yet
```

To insert a word, start at the root and follow its letters one by one, creating each child that is missing. Then mark the last node:

```javascript harness
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.children = new Map();
    this.isEnd = false;
  }
}
const root = new TrieNode();
const word = "cat";
{{code}}
console.log([...root.children.keys()], root.children.get("c").children.get("a").children.get("t").isEnd);
```

```javascript
let node = root;
for (const ch of word) {
  if (!node.children.has(ch)) {
    node.children.set(ch, new TrieNode());
  }
  node = node.children.get(ch);
}
node.isEnd = true;
```

Searching walks the same path but gives up at the first missing letter. If the walk succeeds, the text is a **prefix** of some stored word, and it is a stored **word** only if the last node has `isEnd` set. After inserting `cart`, the prefix `car` exists, but `car` is not a word until you insert it too. Both checks cost one step per letter, however many words the trie holds.

**The gotcha:** `children.get(ch)` returns `undefined` for a missing letter, not `null`. Check `has(ch)` first, or compare with `undefined`.

example.code:
const input = require("fs").readFileSync(0, "utf8");
const [first, ...queries] = input.trim().split("\n");

class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.children = new Map();
    this.isEnd = false;
  }
}

class Trie {
  constructor() {
    this.root = new TrieNode();
  }

  insert(word) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const ch of word) {
      if (!node.children.has(ch)) {
        node.children.set(ch, new TrieNode());
      }
      node = node.children.get(ch);
    }
    node.isEnd = true;
  }

  find(prefix) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const ch of prefix) {
      if (!node.children.has(ch)) {
        return null;
      }
      node = node.children.get(ch);
    }
    return node;
  }
}

const trie = new Trie();
for (const word of first.trim().split(/\s+/)) {
  trie.insert(word);
}

for (const q of queries) {
  const text = q.trim();
  const node = trie.find(text);
  if (node === null) {
    console.log(`${text}: missing`);
  } else if (node.isEnd) {
    console.log(`${text}: word`);
  } else {
    console.log(`${text}: prefix only`);
  }
}

