Store one more number on every node: `count`, how many inserted words pass through it. Insert adds 1 to each node on the word's path:

```javascript harness
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.children = new Map();
    this.isEnd = false;
    this.count = 0;
  }
}
const root = new TrieNode();
function insert(word) {
  {{code}}
}
for (const w of ["tea", "ten", "to"]) {
  insert(w);
}
console.log(root.children.get("t").count, root.children.get("t").children.get("e").count);
```

```javascript
let node = root;
for (const ch of word) {
  if (!node.children.has(ch)) {
    node.children.set(ch, new TrieNode());
  }
  node = node.children.get(ch);
  node.count++;
}
node.isEnd = true;
```

After inserting `tea`, `ten`, and `to`, the `t` node counts 3 and the `te` node counts 2. Now "how many words start with this prefix?" is a walk down the prefix and one read of `count`, with no need to visit the words below it. A missing letter means 0 words.

A node with `count` 1 lies on exactly one word's path, so the first such node along a word marks the shortest prefix that picks out that word alone.

**The gotcha:** inserting the same word twice counts it twice. When duplicates shouldn't count, check whether the word is already there before you insert it.

